<worksheet>
<title>Graphing Quadratic Functions</title>
<page>
<objectives>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            In this section we will see how to:
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <p>
                Graph a quadratic equation in standard form.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Graph a quadratic equation in factored form.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Convert a quadratic equation into vertex form.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Graph a quadratic equation in vertex form.
            </p>
        </li>
    </ul>
</objectives>
<p>
    The graph of a quadratic function is called a <term>parabola</term> and it looks like this:
</p>
<figure xml:id="parabola">
        <caption>The graph of a parabola</caption>
        <image width="50%">
        <shortdescription>The graph of the function y=x^2</shortdescription>
        <prefigure label="pf-97g3" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)">
          <definition> f(x) = x^2</definition>
          <coordinates bbox="(-6,-6,6,6)">
            <grid-axes bounding-box="yes"/>
            <graph function="f"/>
            </coordinates>
          </diagram>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    </figure>
    </page>
<page>
    <p>
        Similar to how we graph linear equation (line), we can graph several points and connect them to make the above shape. 
    </p>
    <example>
        <p>
            Graph the quadratic function <m>f(x) = x^2+4x+3</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
            Evaluate the function at several points and then plot those points. When you have enough points to see the outline of a parabola, connect them to complete the graph.
        </p>
        <p>
            <m>f(1) = 8</m>, <m>f(0) = 3</m>, <m>f(-1) = 0</m>, <m>f(-2) = -1</m>, <m>f(-3) = 0</m>, <m>f(-4) = 3</m>, <m>f(-5) = 8</m>
        </p>
        <sidebyside widths = "45% 10% 45%">
           <image width="75%">
        <shortdescription>points on the graph of y = x^2+4x+3</shortdescription>
        <prefigure label="pf-97g4" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)">
          <coordinates bbox="(-10,-10,10,10)">
            <grid-axes spacings="((-9,1,9),(-9,1,9))" hticks="(-9,1,9)" hlabels="(-8,2,8)" vticks="(-9,1,9)" vlabels="(-8,2,8)" bounding-box="yes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
            <point p="(1,8)"/>
                <point p="(0,3)"/>
                    <point p="(-1,0)"/>
                        <point p="(-2,-1)"/>
                            <point p="(-3,0)"/>
                                <point p="(-4,3)"/>
                                    <point p="(-5,8)"/>
            </coordinates>
          </diagram>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    <p>
        
    </p>
    <image width="75%">
        <shortdescription>the graph of y = x^2+4x+3</shortdescription>
        <prefigure label="pf-97g5" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)">
        <definition> f(x) =x^2+4*x+3 </definition>
          <coordinates bbox="(-10,-10,10,10)">
            <grid-axes spacings="((-9,1,9),(-9,1,9))" hticks="(-9,1,9)" hlabels="(-8,2,8)" vticks="(-9,1,9)" vlabels="(-8,2,8)" bounding-box="yes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
            <point p="(1,8)"/>
                <point p="(0,3)"/>
                    <point p="(-1,0)"/>
                        <point p="(-2,-1)"/>
                            <point p="(-3,0)"/>
                                <point p="(-4,3)"/>
                                    <point p="(-5,8)"/>
                                        <graph function="f"/>
            </coordinates>
          </diagram>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    </sidebyside>
    </example>
    <p>
        Notice that the point at <m>x=0</m> is easy to evaluate and is the <m>y</m>-intercept! In standard form, the constant on the end is the <m>y</m>-intercept and is
        a good place to start your graph.
    </p>
    <p>
        Two other points that are easy to graph are the <m>x</m>-intercepts. These are easiest to find if the equation is in factored form. We also saw in a previous section,
        that the vertex is the midpoint between the two <m>x-</m>intercepts, so it can be easily added to the graph after we plot the <m>x</m>-intercepts.
    </p>
    <example>
    <p>
        Graph the quadratic function <m>f(x)=\frac{1}{2}(x-4)(x+2)</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
        The <m>x</m>-intercepts are <m>x=4</m> and <m>x=-2</m>. The midpoint between <m>x=4</m> and <m>x=-2</m> is <m>x=1</m>. Evaluate <m>f(1) =\frac{1}{2}(1-4)(1+2) = -\frac{9}{2}</m>.
        One more easy point to graph is the <m>y-</m>-intercept. <m>f(0) = \frac{1}{2}(0-4)(0+2) = -4</m>
    </p>
    <image width="50%">
        <shortdescription>the graph of y = 1/2(x-4)(x+2)</shortdescription>
        <prefigure label="pf-97g6" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)">
        <definition> f(x) =0.5*(x-4)*(x+2) </definition>
          <coordinates bbox="(-10,-10,10,10)">
            <grid-axes spacings="((-9,1,9),(-9,1,9))" hticks="(-9,1,9)" hlabels="(-8,2,8)" vticks="(-9,1,9)" vlabels="(-8,2,8)" bounding-box="yes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
            <point p="(4,0)"/>
                <point p="(-2,0)"/>
                    <point p="(0,-4)"/>
                        <point p="(1,-4.5)"/>
                             <graph function="f"/>
            </coordinates>
          </diagram>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    </example>
    <p>
        The key point on a parabola is the vertex. If you know the vertex and one other point, like the <m>y</m>-intercept, you can most easily graph a parabola. A quadratic 
        equation can be written in <term>vertex form</term>, <m>f(x) = a(x-h)^2+k</m>, where the vertex is at the point <m>(h,k)</m>. Note that the negative sign is built into
        the formula so that <m>f(x) = 2(x-4)^2+7</m> has vertex <m>(4,7)</m> and <m>f(x)=2(x+4)^2+7=2(x-(-4))^2+7</m> has vertex <m>(-4,7)</m>.
    </p>
    <example>
        <p>
            Graph the quadratic function <m>f(x)=(x+1)^2-2</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
            This function has vertex at <m>(-1,-2)</m>. Evaluate <m>f(0) = (0+1)^2-2 = -1</m> giving us a second point <m>(0,-1)</m>. 
        </p>
        <image width="50%">
        <shortdescription>the graph of y = 1/2(x-4)(x+2)</shortdescription>
        <prefigure label="pf-97g7" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)">
        <definition> f(x) =(x+1)^2-2 </definition>
          <coordinates bbox="(-10,-10,10,10)">
            <grid-axes spacings="((-9,1,9),(-9,1,9))" hticks="(-9,1,9)" hlabels="(-8,2,8)" vticks="(-9,1,9)" vlabels="(-8,2,8)" bounding-box="yes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
            <point p="(-1,-2)"/>
                <point p="(0,-1)"/>
                    <graph function="f"/>
            </coordinates>
          </diagram>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    </example>
    <p>
        The simplest way to graph a quadratic function is to know its vertex and a second point. How do we convert a quadratic equation from standard form to vertex form?
        We factor out a GCF and complete the square! 
    </p>
    <example>
    <statement>
        <p>
           Convert <m>f(x) = -2x^2-8x+2</m> 
        </p>
    </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                <m>f(x) =-2(x+2)^2+10</m>
            </p>
        </answer>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Factor out the GFC of -2. <m>f(x) = -2x^2-8x+2=-2(x^2+4x-1)</m>. Complete the square inside the parentheses. <m>f(x)=-2(x^2+4x+4-4-1) = -2((x+2)^2-5)</m>. 
                Redistribute the -2 to get the vertex form. <m>f(x)=-2(x+2)^2+10</m>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>
    </page>
<page>
    <p>
        <alert>Try it Now!</alert>
    </p>
    <exercise>
        <introduction>
        <p>
            Convert the following quadratic functions to vertex form.
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task workspace="1.5in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>f(x)=x^2+8x+15</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="1.5in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>f(x)=4x^2-4x+1</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="1.5in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>f(x)=-x^2+6x-13</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>
    </page>
<page>
    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Sketch an accurate graph of the following quadratic functions.
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>f(x)=x^2-4x-5</m>
                </p>
            
            <image width="50%">
        <shortdescription>a blank x-y axes</shortdescription>
        <prefigure label="pf-97g8" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)">
         <coordinates bbox="(-10,-10,10,10)">
            <grid-axes spacings="((-9,1,9),(-9,1,9))" hticks="(-9,1,9)" hlabels="(-8,2,8)" vticks="(-9,1,9)" vlabels="(-8,2,8)" bounding-box="yes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
            </coordinates>
          </diagram>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
      </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>f(x)=\frac{1}{3}(x+3)(x-5)</m>
                </p>
            
            <image width="50%">
        <shortdescription>a blank x-y axes</shortdescription>
        <prefigure label="pf-97g9" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)">
         <coordinates bbox="(-10,-10,10,10)">
            <grid-axes spacings="((-9,1,9),(-9,1,9))" hticks="(-9,1,9)" hlabels="(-8,2,8)" vticks="(-9,1,9)" vlabels="(-8,2,8)" bounding-box="yes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
            </coordinates>
          </diagram>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
      </statement>
        </task>
         <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>f(x)=(x+3)^2-2</m>
                </p>
            
            <image width="50%">
        <shortdescription>a blank x-y axes</shortdescription>
        <prefigure label="pf-97g10" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)">
         <coordinates bbox="(-10,-10,10,10)">
            <grid-axes spacings="((-9,1,9),(-9,1,9))" hticks="(-9,1,9)" hlabels="(-8,2,8)" vticks="(-9,1,9)" vlabels="(-8,2,8)" bounding-box="yes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
            </coordinates>
          </diagram>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
      </statement>
        </task>
         <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>f(x)=2(x-4)^2-3</m>
                </p>
           
            <image width="50%">
        <shortdescription>a blank x-y axes</shortdescription>
        <prefigure label="pf-97g11" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)">
         <coordinates bbox="(-10,-10,10,10)">
            <grid-axes spacings="((-9,1,9),(-9,1,9))" hticks="(-9,1,9)" hlabels="(-8,2,8)" vticks="(-9,1,9)" vlabels="(-8,2,8)" bounding-box="yes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
            </coordinates>
          </diagram>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
        
         </statement>
         </task>
    </exercise>
    </page>
</worksheet>